<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Crete</h1><p class="st-meta">1211 CE – 1669 CE · capital Candia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venice's most important overseas colony for over four centuries, where Greek and Italian culture blended to produce El Greco and the Cretan Renaissance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> bought the claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> in 1204. The Genoese adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-pescatore">Enrico Pescatore</a> occupied the island first, and Venice only took control around 1211-1212, sending colonists who received land in return for military service. The capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a>, gave its name to the colony.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Venetian duke and councillors governed, and Venetian settlers formed a feudal nobility over a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> peasantry whose church was subordinated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Frequent revolts, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-saint-titus">Revolt of Saint Titus</a> of 1363, were crushed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> exported wine (malmsey), oil and grain. In the 16th and early 17th centuries Greek-Italian culture flourished: the Cretan school of icon painting, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-greco">El Greco</a> and the romance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erotokritos">Erotokritos</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> invaded in 1645 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> for more than twenty years, one of the longest sieges in history. Candia surrendered in 1669, ending Venetian rule except for a few island forts.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/venetian-crete.jpg" alt="Venetian Crete" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Marco Boschini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MBoschini_Il_regno_tutto_di_Candia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1211 CE – 1669 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Candia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Candia, Regno di Candia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1440&amp;focus=venetian-crete" aria-label="Venetian Crete on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5316 901 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venetian Crete lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5636 1157L5293 831M5636 1157L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5293 831h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1440&amp;focus=venetian-crete">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

